window.RIOT = {"generated_for": "riot.weimar", "preview": false, "note": "Weimar 29–33 — ten recorded votes of the Reichstag's final years, hand-authored from the stenographic record (Verhandlungen des Reichstags) and the literature of record. A memorial deck. Source of truth + audit trail: data/weimar/cards.json + research.json.", "n_decisions": 10, "sessions_in_table": ["RT4", "RT5", "RT6", "RT8"], "parties": [{"token": "SPD", "name": "SPD", "color": "#C8242E", "logo": null}, {"token": "Z", "name": "Zentrum", "color": "#4A4036", "logo": null}, {"token": "BVP", "name": "BVP", "color": "#2C5F8A", "logo": null}, {"token": "DDP", "name": "DDP / Staatspartei", "color": "#C99700", "logo": null}, {"token": "DVP", "name": "DVP", "color": "#B86A1F", "logo": null}, {"token": "DNVP", "name": "DNVP", "color": "#1F3864", "logo": null}, {"token": "NSDAP", "name": "NSDAP", "color": "#5C4023", "logo": null}, {"token": "KPD", "name": "KPD", "color": "#7E1020", "logo": null}], "decisions": [{"id": "DE-1929-freiheitsgesetz", "session_code": "RT4", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1929-11-30", "title": "Gesetz gegen die Versklavung des Deutschen Volkes („Freiheitsgesetz“)", "type": "Volksbegehren bill", "proposed_by": "Reichsausschuß für das deutsche Volksbegehren (DNVP / NSDAP / Stahlhelm)", "doc_ref": "4. Wahlperiode · 30 Nov 1929", "topic": "War guilt", "headline": "Renounce Germany's signed admission of guilt for the World War, demand the foreign troops still on the Rhine leave now — and send to prison for treason any minister who signs Germany up to keep paying the war's victors.", "source_brief": "A citizens' petition — signed by just over the **one-tenth of voters** the constitution requires — forces this bill onto parliament's floor. It declares the Versailles treaty's war-guilt clause (the line where Germany accepted blame for the World War) renounced, demands the occupied Rhineland be cleared, rejects any new payment burdens — and, the sharpest edge, makes it **high treason, punished with prison**, for ministers and officials to co-operate in collecting reparations, the payments Germany owes the war's victors.\n\n- **The money behind it:** Germany has paid since the defeat, and the freshly negotiated schedule now on Europe's table would keep the payments running **until 1988**.\n- **The legal edge is the treason paragraph:** the rest of the bill declares; this part criminalises. Ministers doing their treaty duty today would be defendants tomorrow.\n- **Rejecting it doesn't end it:** under the constitution, a petition bill the chamber votes down goes automatically to a **national referendum** — the campaign continues either way.\n- **The handle:** for — eleven years of paying for a dictated peace is enough, and only a hard stop forces real renegotiation; against — a state cannot threaten its own government with prison for honouring signed agreements, and a one-sided default invites credit collapse and sanctions, not relief.", "deep_facts": "- Brought under Article 73 of the constitution: a Volksbegehren (citizens' initiative) signed by **10.02%** of eligible voters between 16 and 29 October 1929 — just above the 10% threshold that obliges the Reichstag to vote on the bill (de.wikipedia, Volksentscheid gegen den Young-Plan).\n- The bill's first paragraphs renounce the war-guilt acknowledgement resting on **Article 231** of the Treaty of Versailles, demand the immediate evacuation of the occupied areas, and repudiate the acceptance of new reparation burdens; **§4** threatens ministers and officials who sign such obligations with Zuchthaus — penitentiary — for treason (de.wikipedia, Volksentscheid gegen den Young-Plan; Verhandlungen des Reichstags, Bd. 426).\n- The renegotiated reparations schedule agreed by the international experts' conference in 1929 stretches annuities to a final instalment in **1988** (LeMO/DHM, Young-Plan).\n- Allied troops have stood in the Rhineland since the armistice; the Versailles timetable allows the occupation to run until **1935** (LeMO/DHM; de.wikipedia, Young-Plan).\n- The chamber debated the bill on 29–30 November 1929 and took the second reading **paragraph by paragraph** (weimarer-republik.net, November 1929; Verhandlungen des Reichstags, Bd. 426).\n- A bill rejected by the Reichstag after a successful Volksbegehren proceeds to a binding national referendum; because this bill is treated as touching the constitution, passing it there requires the consent of a **majority of all eligible voters**, not just of those who turn out (de.wikipedia, Volksentscheid gegen den Young-Plan).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The operative paragraph is the criminal one: the declarations Germany cannot enforce unilaterally, but §4 would chill every future negotiation by making the negotiators legally disposable — the target is the conference table, not the courtroom.\n- The petition route guarantees a second act: whatever the chamber does, a nationwide referendum campaign follows, so the floor vote is partly a stage — a device to force every deputy onto a public list of 'fulfilment politicians' for the posters.\n- The campaign fuses respectable nationalism's money and newspapers with the street movements' energy; the legitimacy can flow the other way, too — the louder, radical partner stands to pocket the respectability.\n- A default by declaration would not stop the payments machinery, which runs on international credit and enforcement; what it would certainly change is domestic politics, by redefining treaty compliance as betrayal of the nation.", "raw_outcome": "Zweite Lesung des Gesetzentwurfs „Gesetz gegen die Versklavung des Deutschen Volkes“ („Freiheitsgesetz“), paragraphenweise Einzelabstimmung", "source_url": "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volksentscheid_gegen_den_Young-Plan", "outcome": "rejected", "tally": null, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "against", "Z": "against", "BVP": "against", "DDP": "against", "DVP": "against", "DNVP": "for", "NSDAP": "for", "KPD": "against"}}, {"id": "DE-1930-young-plan", "session_code": "RT4", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1930-03-12", "title": "Gesetz über das Abkommen von Den Haag vom 20. Januar 1930 (Young-Plan-Gesetze)", "type": "Ratification law", "proposed_by": "Reichsregierung (Kabinett Müller II)", "doc_ref": "4. Wahlperiode · 12 Mar 1930", "topic": "Reparations", "headline": "Ratify the new war-payments deal struck at The Hague: yearly payments to the war's victors are cut, the foreign supervisor of German finances goes home, occupying troops leave the Rhineland five years early — and the payment schedule runs until 1988.", "source_brief": "Since the defeat Germany has paid reparations — payments for the war's damage — under a plan supervised from outside: a foreign agent sits over German transfers, with railways and industry pledged as security. The settlement on the table replaces all of it: **lower yearly payments**, transfers handled by Germany itself through a new international bank, **foreign supervision abolished** — and the occupying troops leave the Rhineland **this summer**, five years before the treaty obliges them to.\n\n- **The relief is real:** the first years cost about **a third less** than the current schedule, and the controls — the foreign payments agent, the pledged railways — are wound up.\n- **The price is time:** the schedule runs to **1988**, fifty-nine more years. And the old plan's safety valve shrinks: a fixed share of each year's payment becomes unconditional — owed in full even in a slump.\n- **The handle:** for — pay less, govern your own money, get your own soil back, all without firing a shot; against — signing accepts the defeat's bill for two more generations, with its hardest core due precisely when crisis makes paying impossible.", "deep_facts": "- Drawn up by an international committee of financial experts meeting in Paris from February 1929, and finalised at the Hague conferences of August 1929 and January 1930; the ratification law covers the agreement signed at The Hague on **20 January 1930** (LeMO/DHM, Young-Plan; de.wikipedia, Young-Plan).\n- Annuities average about **2.0 billion Reichsmark**, starting near 1.7 billion and rising over the schedule, against the standard annuity of **2.5 billion** under the current Dawes arrangement; the final instalment falls due in **1988** (LeMO/DHM, Young-Plan; de.wikipedia, Young-Plan).\n- Foreign controls end: the office of the Agent General for Reparation Payments is abolished and the securities pledged on the state railways and industry are released; a newly created **Bank for International Settlements** in Basel handles the transfers (LeMO/DHM, Young-Plan; de.wikipedia, Young-Plan).\n- Transfer protection narrows: under the Dawes arrangement payments abroad could be suspended in a crisis; under the new plan a fixed unconditional tranche of each annuity must be paid regardless, and only the remainder can be postponed, for at most two years (de.wikipedia, Young-Plan).\n- In exchange the Rhineland occupation ends by **30 June 1930** — the Versailles treaty would have allowed it to run to 1935 (LeMO/DHM, Jahreschronik 1930; Historisches Lexikon Bayerns, Young-Plan).\n- The package before the chamber comprises several laws taken in the same sitting; this is the third reading, after committee passage and a second reading the previous day (Gonschior, Chronik 1928–30; weimarer-republik.net, März 1930).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The settlement trades amount for certainty: lower annuities are bought by surrendering the suspension valve. The design works if the economy grows — in a slump, the unconditional core bites hardest exactly when paying is hardest.\n- The early evacuation converts a financial schedule into a sovereignty prize that can be shown to voters now; the payments stretch two generations. The gain is front-loaded to this government, the bill is inherited by every one that follows.\n- Ending foreign supervision does not end enforcement — it relocates it. The discipline moves from occupation and pledged railways to the credit markets: a Germany that must keep borrowing abroad polices its own compliance.\n- Sixty-year schedules assume sixty years of political continuity. The plan's deepest wager is not financial but constitutional: that the state signing it will still be there, and still willing, in 1988.", "raw_outcome": "Dritte Lesung der Gesetze über das Abkommen von Den Haag vom 20. Januar 1930 (Young-Plan-Gesetze)", "source_url": "https://www.dhm.de/lemo/jahreschronik/1930", "outcome": "approved", "tally": {"for": 270, "against": 192, "abstain": 0}, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "for", "Z": "for", "BVP": "against", "DDP": "for", "DVP": "for", "DNVP": "against", "NSDAP": "against", "KPD": "against"}}, {"id": "DE-1930-deckungsvorlage", "session_code": "RT4", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1930-07-16", "title": "Deckungsvorlage der Reichsregierung (Gesetzentwurf zur Deckung des Reichshaushalts 1930)", "type": "Government bill", "proposed_by": "Reichsregierung (Kabinett Brüning I)", "doc_ref": "4. Wahlperiode · 16 Jul 1930", "topic": "Austerity", "headline": "Close the Reich's budget hole with austerity: create a new tax on every adult, take a slice off public-sector salaries, raise the wage deduction for unemployment insurance, and cut state spending — instead of borrowing more.", "source_brief": "The slump has blown a hole in the Reich budget: tax receipts are falling while millions draw unemployment support. The government — in office without a parliamentary majority since spring — proposes to cover the deficit by **taking more and spending less**. And it has told the chamber plainly what follows a no: the same measures by **presidential emergency decree** under Article 48, the president's power to rule without parliament.\n\n- **Who pays:** every adult, through a new per-head 'citizen tax'; public servants, through a special levy on their salaries; insured workers, through unemployment contributions rising to **4.5%** of wages.\n- **Why now:** the unemployment-insurance fund is bleeding and the Reich must keep topping it up; without cover, the deficit rolls forward as short-term debt the Reich already finds hard and expensive to raise.\n- **The handle:** for — balance the books through parliament, on parliament's terms and with parliament's amendments, rather than hand the budget pen to the palace; against — the package reaches for wage-earners, the insured and flat per-head taxes while property and big capital go lightly, and squeezing a contracting economy can deepen the very slump that opened the hole.", "deep_facts": "- The cover package combines new revenue and cuts: a **Bürgersteuer** (a per-head citizen tax, graduated by income, levied for the municipalities), a special solidarity levy on public-sector and other fixed salaries (the 'Notopfer der Festbesoldeten'), a rise in the unemployment-insurance contribution to **4.5%** of wages with tightened benefits, and reductions on the spending side (Akten der Reichskanzlei 1930, Sanierungspolitik, bundesarchiv.de).\n- The Reich budget for 1930 carries a deficit driven by collapsing tax receipts and rising subsidies to the unemployment-insurance fund; registered unemployment passed **three million** in the winter of 1929/30 (LeMO/DHM, Jahreschronik 1930; Akten der Reichskanzlei).\n- The cabinet has governed without a coalition majority since the end of March 1930, when the previous government broke apart over how to fund unemployment insurance (LeMO/DHM, Jahreschronik 1930).\n- **Article 48** of the constitution lets the Reich president enact measures with the force of law when public security and order are seriously endangered; under its third paragraph the Reichstag can demand their repeal by majority vote (Weimarer Reichsverfassung, Art. 48; documentArchiv.de).\n- The chancellor declared on the floor, before the division, that the government would resort to Article 48 if the cover bill failed (Akten der Reichskanzlei, Deckungsvorlagen und politische Lage, bundesarchiv.de).\n- The bill was already a fragile compromise inside the cabinet's own camp: the planned salary levy drew open resistance from civil-service organisations, and coalition-adjacent groups pressed to swap the new levies for a higher turnover tax (Akten der Reichskanzlei 1930, Sanierungspolitik, bundesarchiv.de).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- This is a budget bill doubling as a constitutional test. The announced decree route converts the question from 'this package, yes or no' into 'does parliament still write the budget at all': a yes binds the chamber to austerity it barely shaped; a no concedes the pen — and sets a precedent that the chamber is dispensable for the state's core business.\n- The chosen instruments — a flat per-head tax, a salary levy, higher wage-based contributions — sit heaviest on steady earners and the insured. The tilt reads less like doctrine than negotiation residue: property- and consumption-based alternatives were bargained away before the bill reached the floor.\n- The economics cut against themselves: raising levies and cutting spending into a contraction shrinks demand and, with it, the next year's tax receipts. The wager is that balanced books restore foreign credit faster than the squeeze deepens the slump.\n- A government that survives by threatening the emergency power has already half-moved into it; the threat works once as leverage, then becomes the routine.", "raw_outcome": "Abstimmung über die Deckungsvorlage der Reichsregierung zur Deckung des Reichshaushalts 1930", "source_url": "https://www.weimarer-republik.net/en/weimar-gateway/timeline-of-the-weimar-republic/1930/july-1930/", "outcome": "rejected", "tally": {"for": 193, "against": 256, "abstain": 0}, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "against", "Z": "for", "BVP": "for", "DDP": "for", "DVP": "for", "DNVP": "against", "NSDAP": "against", "KPD": "against"}}, {"id": "DE-1930-art48-revolt", "session_code": "RT4", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1930-07-18", "title": "Antrag auf Aufhebung der Verordnung des Reichspräsidenten zur Behebung finanzieller, wirtschaftlicher und sozialer Notstände vom 16. Juli 1930 (Art. 48 Abs. 3 der Reichsverfassung)", "type": "Decree-repeal motion (Article 48 §3)", "proposed_by": "SPD parliamentary group (Reichstag faction)", "doc_ref": "Reichstag, IV. Wahlperiode, sitting of 18 July 1930 — Antrag nach Art. 48 Abs. 3 WRV auf Aufhebung der Notverordnung vom 16. Juli 1930", "topic": "Emergency powers", "headline": "Strike down the President's emergency decree — signed under Article 48, his rule-by-decree power — that raises taxes and cuts spending without a parliamentary vote.", "source_brief": "Germany is deep in the world slump and the Reich budget has a hole in it. The government's answer — higher taxes, lower spending — has become law not by parliament's vote but by the President's signature, under Article 48, his emergency rule-by-decree power. The constitution leaves the chamber one counter-move: demand the decree's repeal, and it must be scrapped. That demand is what you're voting on.\n\n- **What dies if you vote for:** the decree of 16 July — the whole austerity package, tax rises and spending cuts, enacted by signature instead of by vote.\n- **The principle:** the budget is parliament's oldest power. If a government can decree what the chamber declines to pass, every future 'no' on the floor can be overridden from above.\n- **The price:** the President also holds the power to dissolve parliament. Strike his decree down and the widely expected answer is dissolution — fresh elections in the middle of mass unemployment, a gamble for every party in the room.\n- **The handle:** for — a parliament that won't defend its own budget right has stopped being one; against — the deficit is real, the measures arguably unavoidable, and forcing an election in a slump may strengthen exactly the forces that want this chamber gone.", "deep_facts": "- **Article 48(2)** of the Weimar constitution lets the Reich President take 'the necessary measures' with the force of law when public security and order are seriously endangered; **Article 48(3)** obliges him to notify the Reichstag at once and to revoke the measures 'auf Verlangen des Reichstags' — at the chamber's demand, by simple majority (Weimarer Reichsverfassung, Art. 48).\n- The decree under vote, the *Verordnung zur Behebung finanzieller, wirtschaftlicher und sozialer Notstände*, was signed on **16 July 1930** and reproduces in substance the deficit-covering bill (*Deckungsvorlage*) the government had put before the chamber (weimarer-republik.net, July 1930; Gonschior, Deutschland 1928–30).\n- Its content is the government's deficit-covering programme for the 1930 Reich budget: tax increases and spending cuts (weimarer-republik.net, July 1930; LeMO Jahreschronik 1930).\n- The cabinet in office since **30 March 1930** is the Republic's first 'presidential cabinet': formed without a parliamentary coalition behind it after the last majority government collapsed that month (LeMO Jahreschronik 1930).\n- President Ebert invoked Article 48 more than **130 times** in the Republic's crisis years up to 1925; the article had since fallen largely out of use (en.wikipedia, Article 48 of the Weimar Constitution).\n- **Article 25** gives the President the separate power to dissolve the Reichstag — 'jedoch nur einmal aus dem gleichen Anlaß' (only once for the same cause) — with new elections within sixty days (Weimarer Reichsverfassung, Art. 25).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The repeal right of Article 48(3) is parliament's only enforcement tool against decree rule, but it is wired to a tripwire: using it invites Article 25, so the chamber can defend its budget power only by betting its own existence.\n- Whichever way the division falls, the precedent that a rejected finance bill can simply be re-enacted by decree shifts the working constitution: the chamber's 'no' stops being final, and bargaining power moves from the floor to the President's anteroom.\n- Any majority for repeal is necessarily a marriage of opposites — deputies who want this decree gone for social reasons and deputies who want the parliamentary system itself discredited would have to vote the same word; a win for the motion is therefore not a win for any one programme.\n- For the government, losing may be the less bad outcome: dissolution resets the chamber while the decree machinery keeps running — emergency rule does not pause for an election campaign.", "raw_outcome": "Dem Antrag auf Aufhebung der Notverordnung mit 236 gegen 221 Stimmen zugestimmt (FES Chronik der deutschen Sozialdemokratie; Gonschior: 236 zu 222)", "source_url": "http://library.fes.de/fulltext/bibliothek/chronik/band2/e235f784.html", "outcome": "approved", "tally": {"for": 236, "against": 221, "abstain": 0}, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "for", "Z": "against", "BVP": "against", "DDP": "against", "DVP": "against", "NSDAP": "for", "KPD": "for"}}, {"id": "DE-1930-toleration", "session_code": "RT5", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1930-10-18", "title": "Antrag, der Reichsregierung das Vertrauen zu entziehen (Misstrauensantrag gegen das Kabinett Brüning), verbunden mit Anträgen auf Aufhebung der Notverordnungen", "type": "No-confidence motion", "proposed_by": "NSDAP, KPD and DNVP parliamentary groups (separate motions)", "doc_ref": "Reichstag, V. Wahlperiode, first working session, sittings of 13–18 October 1930 (division in the early hours of 19 October)", "topic": "Confidence in the government", "headline": "Bring down the Chancellor's cabinet: declare that the newly elected parliament has no confidence in the government ruling the slump by emergency decree, forcing its resignation on the spot.", "source_brief": "The chamber elected in September meets for its first real working week, and the first item of substance is the government's head. The cabinet has been running the country by presidential emergency decree — Article 48, rule by the President's signature — and holds nowhere near a majority of its own. Motions on the table demand that parliament withdraw its confidence, which under the constitution forces the cabinet's resignation; sister motions would scrap its decrees with it.\n\n- **The new arithmetic:** September's election made the National Socialists the second-largest party — **107 seats**, up from 12 — and gave the Communists **77**; both fought the campaign against the Republic itself, and together they hold roughly a third of the chamber.\n- **What a yes sets off:** the cabinet falls, no alternative majority is in sight, and fresh elections in a deepening slump are the realistic sequel — a second roll of the dice, weeks after the first came up radical.\n- **What a no means:** a Chancellor who answers to the President rather than the chamber stays on, and austerity keeps arriving by decree; parliament's remaining role shrinks to deciding, periodically, not to stop him.\n- **The handle:** for — no parliament worth the name leaves a government it never chose ruling by signature; against — bring it down now and the next campaign belongs to the parties that want the building, not the budget.", "deep_facts": "- The election of **14 September 1930** returned a chamber of **577 seats**: NSDAP 107 (up from 12), KPD 77, SPD 143 — still the largest party — with the parties of the governing middle losing ground across the board (en.wikipedia, 1930 German federal election).\n- **Article 54** of the constitution: the Chancellor and every minister require the Reichstag's confidence for the conduct of office and 'must resign if the Reichstag withdraws confidence by explicit resolution' (Weimarer Reichsverfassung, Art. 54).\n- The cabinet, in office since **30 March 1930**, is the Republic's first formed without a parliamentary coalition; it rests on the President's backing and has enacted its economic programme over the summer through Article 48 emergency decrees — the President's rule-by-decree power — including the consolidated decree of **26 July 1930** (LeMO Jahreschronik 1930; slpb, Die Kanzlerschaft Brünings).\n- At the constitutive sitting of **13 October** the 107 National Socialist deputies enter the chamber in party uniform (slpb, Die Kanzlerschaft Brünings).\n- The closing sitting of the opening week runs some **fourteen hours**, through the night of 18–19 October (en.wikipedia, October 1930).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The motion's real addressee is not the cabinet but the chamber's democratic majority: the question underneath is whether normal opposition is still affordable when the only majority that could replace this government is one that wants no government answerable to parliament at all.\n- A cabinet that survives confidence tests without ever winning a positive majority turns Article 54 into a one-way ratchet — the chamber can keep governments alive but no longer makes them — and each survived division normalises decree rule a little further.\n- For the radical movers, losing the division may serve as well as winning it: every vote that keeps the cabinet alive through forbearance rather than support is campaign material against the system itself.\n- Whoever tips this division decides more than a chancellorship: it settles whether September's verdict is read as a mandate to stop the machine, or as a warning never again to consult the voters in a slump.", "raw_outcome": "Misstrauensanträge abgelehnt; die Vertrauensabstimmung geht 318 zu 236 für die Regierung aus (en.wikipedia Tageschronik; deutsche Chroniken bestätigen Ablehnung ohne Zahlen)", "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/October_1930", "outcome": "rejected", "tally": {"for": 236, "against": 318, "abstain": 0}, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "against", "Z": "against", "BVP": "against", "DDP": "against", "DVP": "against", "DNVP": "for", "NSDAP": "for", "KPD": "for"}}, {"id": "DE-1931-geschaeftsordnung", "session_code": "RT5", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1931-02-09", "title": "Abänderung der Geschäftsordnung für den Reichstag (Deckungsvorlage bei Finanzanträgen, Einschränkung der Immunität)", "type": "Standing-orders reform", "proposed_by": "Cross-party motion of the majority factions (inter-party conference of 4 February 1931)", "doc_ref": "Reichstag, V. Wahlperiode, sitting of 9 February 1931 — interfraktioneller Antrag vom 4. Februar 1931", "topic": "Parliamentary procedure", "headline": "Tighten parliament's own rulebook against obstruction: motions that spend money or repeal emergency decrees must say how they're paid for, and immunity narrows for deputies facing criminal cases.", "source_brief": "Parliament is being worked to a standstill from inside. Since the new chamber met, the order paper has filled with serial demonstration motions — demands to repeal the President's emergency decrees, bids to spend money with no source named — each one forcing the house to stop, debate and vote. The proposal rewrites the Reichstag's own rulebook to make that game costly.\n\n- **The new rules:** a motion that raises spending or cuts revenue must name where the money comes from before it reaches the floor; motions to repeal emergency decrees are tied to the same discipline; and immunity protection narrows for deputies with criminal cases pending.\n- **Why now:** September's election filled the chamber with deputies who treat its procedures as a stage — keeping the house from working is, for them, a result in itself, and the motions have multiplied accordingly.\n- **The catch:** these rules are written by a majority to quiet opponents that millions of voters just sent in. Muting them on paper doesn't make them disappear; it may simply move the fight from the floor to the street.\n- **The handle:** for — without working rules there is soon no parliament left to obstruct; against — a majority that rewrites the rulebook to disarm an opposition it cannot out-argue teaches every future majority to do the same.", "deep_facts": "- The reform originates in an inter-party conference of faction representatives on **4 February 1931**; the agreed motion reaches the floor five days later (Akten der Reichskanzlei, Kabinett Brüning I, 'Abänderung der Geschäftsordnung für den Reichstag').\n- Two instruments are targeted: **finance motions** — under the new text, motions to raise expenditure or reduce revenue require an accompanying funding proposal (*Deckungsvorlage*) — and **deputy immunity**, which is narrowed for members with pending criminal proceedings (Akten der Reichskanzlei, ibid.).\n- The repeal channel being disciplined sits in **Article 48(3)** of the constitution: presidential emergency decrees — the President's rule-by-decree power — must be revoked 'at the demand of the Reichstag', so any faction can put a repeal motion to the house at any sitting (Weimarer Reichsverfassung, Art. 48).\n- Since the chamber convened on **13 October 1930**, repeal and censure motions have arrived in series at each block of sittings; chronicles describe the change as aimed at obstruction through repeated motions (LeMO Jahreschronik 1931; weimarer-republik.net, February 1931).\n- The chamber counts **577 deputies** (en.wikipedia, 1930 German federal election).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- An anti-obstruction rule is also self-protection for the chamber's tolerating majority: every forced division on a decree is a night its members must publicly stand next to an austerity they only half-support, so fewer divisions means fewer wounds.\n- Requiring funding cover sounds like book-keeping but works as a filter on opposition speech: the cheapest way to oppose — the symbolic motion — is priced out, while the majority's own business passes untouched.\n- Rules that assume bad faith concede something larger: a standing order can keep a house orderly, but it cannot restore a shared purpose, and a parliament that functions only by restraining its loudest members has already shrunk from arena to administration.\n- The precedent cuts forward: once procedure has been redrawn to manage an unwanted opposition, the next majority — whoever it turns out to be — inherits both the tool and the justification.", "raw_outcome": "Mit 297 Stimmen ohne Gegenstimmen angenommen (Akten der Reichskanzlei: 'Der Reichstag stimmte in der Sitzung vom 9. Februar dem Antrag mit 297 Stimmen ohne Gegenstimmen zu.')", "source_url": "https://apps.bundesarchiv.de/aktenreichskanzlei/1919-1933/1120/bru/bru1p/kap1_2/kap2_234/para3_1.html", "outcome": "approved", "tally": {"for": 297, "against": 0, "abstain": 0}, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "for", "Z": "for", "BVP": "for", "DDP": "for", "DVP": "for"}}, {"id": "DE-1931-harzburg-misstrauen", "session_code": "RT5", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1931-10-16", "title": "Misstrauensanträge gegen das Kabinett Brüning II und Anträge auf Aufhebung der Notverordnungen", "type": "No-confidence motion", "proposed_by": "NSDAP, DNVP and KPD parliamentary groups (separate motions)", "doc_ref": "Verhandlungen des Reichstags, 5. Wahlperiode, Sitzung vom 16. Oktober 1931", "topic": "Confidence in the government", "headline": "Bring down the Chancellor and his cabinet: declare no confidence in a government that has ruled for a year by presidential emergency decree, never through this chamber.", "source_brief": "Germany is in its second year of government by decree. The cabinet has no majority here and does not pretend to: it governs on the President's signature, under Article 48, the constitution's emergency-rule clause, while the chamber stands adjourned for months at a time. Now — days after the nationalist opposition rallied at a Harz spa town to demand the government's head, and with the banks barely propped back up after a summer collapse — the motion on the table would sack the government and strike down its latest decrees with it.\n\n- **What the decrees do:** cut wages, benefits and prices by decree to defend the currency in the middle of a world slump — austerity imposed without a single vote of this house.\n- **A yes means:** the Chancellor and every minister must go. But this splintered chamber has no successor to offer, so the choice of the next government falls to the President and his circle — or to new elections in the depths of the crisis.\n- **The case for the axe:** if a parliament cannot remove a government it never approved, it is decoration. Every month of silent toleration makes decree rule more normal.\n- **The case for holding back:** the cabinet, whatever its method, is the last thing standing between the state and the parties of the street; topple it with no replacement ready and you may get something far worse than austerity.", "deep_facts": "- The cabinet was re-formed on 9–10 October 1931 as Chancellor Heinrich Brüning's second; Brüning now also heads the Foreign Ministry (LeMO Jahreschronik 1931; Akten der Reichskanzlei, Kabinett Brüning II).\n- On 11 October 1931, at Bad Harzburg, the 'national opposition' — NSDAP, DNVP and the Stahlhelm veterans' league, with the Reichslandbund — rallied on Alfred Hugenberg's initiative and demanded the resignation of the Reich government and new elections (LeMO Jahreschronik 1931; 1000dokumente.de, Hauptresolution der Harzburger Tagung).\n- The day before the rally, 10 October 1931, President Hindenburg and the Chancellor received Adolf Hitler for talks, the government hoping cooperation would weaken the pressure of the 'national opposition' (LeMO Jahreschronik 1931).\n- The Reichstag reconvened on 13 October 1931 after standing adjourned since the end of March; the no-confidence and decree-repeal motions were debated for three days (Gonschior, chronicle of the Reich 1931–33; LeMO Jahreschronik 1931).\n- Article 48 of the constitution lets the President issue emergency decrees with the force of law; Article 48(3) lets the Reichstag demand their repeal by simple majority; Article 54 obliges the Chancellor and ministers to resign if the chamber withdraws its confidence; Article 25 lets the President dissolve the chamber (Weimarer Reichsverfassung, Art. 25, 48, 54).\n- On 13 July 1931 the Darmstädter und Nationalbank (Danat-Bank), the Reich's third-largest bank, collapsed after foreign credits were withdrawn, setting off a general banking crisis (LeMO Jahreschronik 1931).\n- Registered unemployment reaches 5.6 million by 31 December 1931 (LeMO Jahreschronik 1931).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The motion is less about this cabinet than about who appoints the next one: with the chamber splintered, removal would transfer the choice to the President and his advisers, not back to parliament — the house can destroy a government, but cannot currently build one.\n- A failed motion still pays its backers: every division forces whichever parties keep the cabinet alive to co-own its wage and benefit cuts in front of their own voters, while those demanding dissolution expect the slump to reward them at the polls.\n- Each survival the government buys this way also normalises the method: a chamber that matters only on the days it threatens to bring everything down, then adjourns for months, is teaching the country that governing happens elsewhere.", "raw_outcome": "Misstrauensanträge gegen das Kabinett Brüning II und Anträge auf Aufhebung der Notverordnungen", "source_url": "https://www.gonschior.de/weimar/Deutschland/1931-33.html", "outcome": "rejected", "tally": {"for": 270, "against": 295, "abstain": 0}, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "against", "Z": "against", "BVP": "against", "DDP": "against", "DVP": "for", "DNVP": "for", "NSDAP": "for", "KPD": "for"}}, {"id": "DE-1932-papen-misstrauen", "session_code": "RT6", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1932-09-12", "title": "Antrag auf Aufhebung der Notverordnungen vom 4. und 5. September 1932 und Entziehung des Vertrauens von der Reichsregierung", "type": "Decree repeal and no-confidence motion", "proposed_by": "KPD parliamentary group", "doc_ref": "Verhandlungen des Reichstags, 6. Wahlperiode, Sitzung vom 12. September 1932", "topic": "Confidence in the government", "headline": "Strike down the emergency decrees that let employers pay below the union-agreed wage, and withdraw parliament's confidence from the unelected 'cabinet of barons' that issued them.", "source_brief": "The new chamber's first working day, and the only real question is whether the government survives it. The cabinet was hand-picked by the President in June — aristocrats, officers and senior officials, barely a party man among them — and it governs by emergency decree under Article 48, the constitution's emergency-rule clause. Its September decrees try to restart the economy by giving firms tax vouchers and the right to undercut union-agreed wages. One motion does both jobs at once: repeal the decrees, and declare this house has no confidence in the government.\n\n- **The decrees on trial:** tax vouchers to lighten business costs, plus permission to cut pay by up to a fifth on part of the working week for employers who take on extra staff — a jobs programme that businesses welcome and the unions call wage theft.\n- **A yes means:** the decrees fall and the cabinet must resign. But the Chancellor has arrived carrying the President's order dissolving this chamber — vote him down and you may simply be sent back to the voters within weeks, decrees intact.\n- **The case against the government:** a cabinet with almost no seats behind it, rewriting wage contracts by decree, is the constitution upside down. If parliament cannot say no to this, it can say no to nothing.\n- **The case for it:** somebody must govern, and this chamber — by its own arithmetic — contains no possible majority for any cabinet. Demolition without a successor means another election in a year of street violence and six-figure dole queues, and the decrees are the first attempt at job creation after years of pure austerity.", "deep_facts": "- The cabinet was appointed on 1 June 1932 by President Hindenburg under Franz von Papen; its ministers are largely non-party aristocrats, officers and officials — the press dubs it the 'cabinet of barons' (LeMO, biography Franz von Papen).\n- The emergency decree of 4 September 1932 aims to lift the economy out of deflation by lowering the burden on businesses with tax vouchers; the supplementary decree of 5 September allows employers to lower wages by up to 20% for the thirty-first through fortieth weekly working hour, provided they actually hire additional workers (weimarer-republik.net, timeline September 1932).\n- Employee organisations attack the September programme as serving business interests; employers' organisations back it (weimarer-republik.net, timeline September 1932).\n- The chamber was elected on 31 July 1932 with 608 seats; the NSDAP holds 230 and the KPD 89 — between them, for the first time, a majority of seats belongs to parties committed against the parliamentary republic (en.wikipedia, July 1932 German federal election).\n- Under Article 54 of the constitution the Chancellor and ministers must resign if the Reichstag withdraws its confidence; under Article 48(3) the chamber can demand repeal of emergency decrees by simple majority; under Article 25 the President may dissolve the chamber (Weimarer Reichsverfassung, Art. 25, 48, 54).\n- Before the sitting the President signed an order dissolving the Reichstag; the Chancellor brings the order, in its red portfolio, to the chamber (Akten der Reichskanzlei, Kabinett von Papen; TIME, 'Germany: Reichstag in Revolt', 1932).\n- The motion's mover asks that it be taken immediately; the president of the chamber lets the division proceed while the Chancellor seeks the floor to announce the dissolution (weimarer-republik.net, timeline September 1932; TIME, 1932).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The motion fuses two questions so they cannot be split: a deputy who only wants the wage decrees gone must also execute the government, and one who would tolerate the cabinet must co-sign pay cuts decreed past parliament — maximum embarrassment is part of the design.\n- A government that answers the chamber with a dissolution order makes the constitutional duel circular: the house removes the cabinet, the President removes the house, and rule by decree continues through the gap — whichever way the division goes, the decision returns to the voters, not to this room.\n- Beneath the wage question sits a message to the President: that no cabinet without a party base can survive contact with this parliament, however its seats are sliced — a warning about every future government formed in the palace rather than in the chamber.", "raw_outcome": "Antrag auf Aufhebung der Notverordnungen vom 4. und 5. September 1932 und Entziehung des Vertrauens von der Reichsregierung", "source_url": "https://www.bpb.de/themen/nationalsozialismus-zweiter-weltkrieg/dossier-nationalsozialismus/39537/zerstoerung-der-demokratie-1930-1932/", "outcome": "approved", "tally": {"for": 512, "against": 42, "abstain": 5}, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "for", "Z": "for", "BVP": "for", "DDP": "for", "DVP": "against", "DNVP": "against", "NSDAP": "for", "KPD": "for"}}, {"id": "DE-1933-ermaechtigung", "session_code": "RT8", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1933-03-23", "title": "Gesetz zur Behebung der Not von Volk und Reich (Ermächtigungsgesetz)", "type": "Constitution-amending law", "proposed_by": "Reich government (Hitler cabinet)", "doc_ref": "Verhandlungen des Reichstags, Bd. 457, Sitzung vom 23. März 1933; RGBl. 1933 I S. 141", "topic": "Constitutional powers", "headline": "Let the cabinet make laws on its own for four years — no vote of parliament needed, and the laws may depart from the constitution itself.", "source_brief": "The chamber, meeting in an opera house because parliament's own building burned a month ago, is asked to hand its lawmaking power to the government. For four years the cabinet could enact any law by itself — including laws that deviate from the constitution — and conclude treaties without asking this house. The government calls it the cure for years of deadlock; the text contains no court, no veto, and almost no exceptions.\n\n- **The case made for it:** the chamber has produced no working majority for years, and governments have legislated by presidential emergency decree anyway — this at least puts a responsible cabinet, not one old man's signature, behind the laws. The powers expire on 1 April 1937, and lapse earlier if this cabinet is replaced. From the rostrum, the government promises to use them 'only in so far as essential' and to leave the chamber, the upper house, the President's rights, the states and the churches untouched.\n- **The case against:** every one of those promises is spoken, not written into the law. Once the house gives lawmaking away it keeps no lever to take it back, however the powers are used — and 'temporary' is policed by no one but the government itself.\n- **The room you vote in:** armed party troops line the corridors and ring the building, dozens of deputies are under arrest or fled, and one party's seats have simply been struck from the register.\n- **The bar:** as a law that changes the constitution it needs two thirds of the house present and two thirds of the votes cast — the constitution's fence around its own text.", "deep_facts": "- The bill has five articles: Reich laws may be enacted by the government as well as by parliament (Art. 1); such laws may deviate from the constitution, so long as they do not touch the institutions of the Reichstag and Reichsrat as such or the rights of the President (Art. 2); laws issued by the government are drawn up by the Chancellor and take effect the day after publication (Art. 3); treaties with foreign states no longer need legislative assent (Art. 4); the law expires on 1 April 1937, and earlier if the present Reich government is replaced by another (Art. 5) (RGBl. 1933 I S. 141; bundestag.de Textarchiv, 23.3.1933).\n- As a constitution-amending law it falls under Article 76: two thirds of the chamber's members must be present, and two thirds of those present must assent (Weimarer Reichsverfassung, Art. 76; bpb, Hintergrund aktuell).\n- The Reichstag building burned on the night of 27–28 February 1933; the sitting takes place in the Kroll Opera House opposite (bundestag.de Textarchiv; LeMO, Ermächtigungsgesetz).\n- The 'Decree of the Reich President for the Protection of People and State' of 28 February 1933, issued under Article 48, the constitution's emergency-rule clause, suspended the core civil rights — personal liberty, free expression, press, assembly — indefinitely; mass arrests of the government's opponents followed (bpb, Hintergrund aktuell; LeMO).\n- The 81 mandates won by the KPD in the 5 March election were annulled; Communist deputies are under arrest or in hiding and take no part in the sitting (bundestag.de Textarchiv; bpb).\n- 26 of the SPD's 120 deputies are missing from the sitting — arrested or fled (de.wikipedia, Ermächtigungsgesetz vom 24. März 1933; bundestag.de Textarchiv).\n- Earlier in the same sitting the chamber changed its standing orders: deputies absent without excuse count as present, so arrests and flight do not break the two-thirds attendance quorum (das-parlament.de, 'Als der Reichstag sich faktisch selbst abschaffte'; de.wikipedia).\n- Uniformed SA and SS men stand in the aisles and corridors and surround the building during the debate (bundestag.de Textarchiv; DHM/LeMO).\n- Moving the bill, the Chancellor assures the chamber the government will use the powers only for vital measures, and that the existence of the Reichstag and Reichsrat, the position of the President, the states and the churches are not threatened (Verhandlungen des Reichstags, Bd. 457).\n- The government coalition formed on 30 January 1933 controls 340 of the 647 elected seats (NSDAP 288, Kampffront Schwarz-Weiß-Rot 52) — a majority, but short of two thirds of the elected chamber (en.wikipedia, March 1933 German federal election).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- The two-thirds rule exists as the constitution's lock on itself; annulled mandates, mass arrests and the new count-the-absent-as-present arithmetic pick that lock before the key is even voted on — a yes ratifies the procedure as much as the text.\n- Every guarantee offered for the bill lives in a speech, not in the five articles: the law names no arbiter, so each promise depends entirely on the promisor's future self-restraint — and a government able to enact any law is also able to redefine what was promised.\n- The sunset clause cuts both ways: a four-year horizon makes assent feel provisional, but a cabinet that can rewrite electoral law, press law and the rules on parties could reshape the very country that would have to decide on renewal — the second decision would not be taken under the conditions of the first.", "raw_outcome": "Gesetz zur Behebung der Not von Volk und Reich (Ermächtigungsgesetz)", "source_url": "https://www.bundestag.de/dokumente/textarchiv/1933-03-23-ermaechtigungsgesetz-938540", "outcome": "approved", "tally": {"for": 444, "against": 94, "abstain": 0}, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "against", "Z": "for", "BVP": "for", "DDP": "for", "DVP": "for", "DNVP": "for", "NSDAP": "for"}}, {"id": "DE-1933-friedensresolution", "session_code": "RT8", "organ": "Reichstag", "point": null, "date": "1933-05-17", "title": "Entschließung des Reichstags zur Regierungserklärung („Friedensresolution“)", "type": "Resolution", "proposed_by": "NSDAP, DNVP, Zentrum and BVP parliamentary groups (joint resolution)", "doc_ref": "Verhandlungen des Reichstags, Bd. 457, Sitzung vom 17. Mai 1933", "topic": "Foreign policy", "headline": "Put parliament behind the government's declaration that Germany wants peace and equal armament rights — and will walk out of the Geneva disarmament talks and the League of Nations if the other powers refuse.", "source_brief": "The Chancellor has just told the chamber — and through it, the world — that the new Germany renounces force, asks only for equality among nations, and will leave the Geneva disarmament conference and the League of Nations, the world body of the day, if equality is denied. The resolution on the table puts this house behind that declaration. The words are the mildest the regime has spoken; the room they are spoken in is not.\n\n- **What a yes endorses:** the declaration itself — peace professed, the armament limits of the Versailles peace treaty applied equally to everyone or to no one, and the threat to quit Geneva and the League if refused.\n- **Why a yes is thinkable for anyone:** equal treatment under Versailles is the one demand every German party has made for a decade. As a text, this could have come from any previous government.\n- **What a yes also does:** it hands a government that jails its critics a world-stage picture of national unity — and abroad, endorsement of the declaration will be read as endorsement of everything else done in its name.\n- **How the vote works:** no ballot papers. Assent is given by standing up, in the open, with dozens of opposition deputies already in prison or in exile — and word has come down from the interior ministry that the lives of the jailed may hang on how the house behaves.", "deep_facts": "- The government declaration of 17 May 1933 addresses the Geneva disarmament conference, in session since February 1932: Germany demands equal rights in armaments, professes peace and rejects war as a means of solving Europe's problems, and announces it would leave the conference and the League of Nations if equality is denied (Verhandlungen des Reichstags, Bd. 457; bundestag.de, WD 1-013/24).\n- Contemporaries register the speech's moderation; the historian Heinrich August Winkler later calls it 'the most measured and peaceable he ever gave' (bundestag.de, WD 1-013/24, citing Winkler).\n- In the week before the sitting, the SPD's party buildings, newspapers and assets were seized (bundestag.de, WD 1-013/24).\n- The Communist deputies are entirely absent — exposed since the Reichstag fire of February to arrest, abduction and mistreatment; parts of the SPD caucus are missing too (bundestag.de, WD 1-013/24).\n- Of the SPD's 120 elected deputies, 65 can still be reached for the caucus meeting of 16 May; the party's executive, partly already in exile in Prague, urges the deputies to stay away from the sitting demonstratively (bundestag.de, WD 1-013/24; FES, Chronik der deutschen Sozialdemokratie).\n- In the council of elders, the Reich interior minister remarks: 'It is a matter of indifference to us whether the Social Democrats assent to the Reichstag's resolutions or not. In any case they may know that to us the life of the nation stands higher than the life of individual men' (Winkler, Der Weg in die Katastrophe, p. 934, via bundestag.de, WD 1-013/24).\n- No roll-call vote is scheduled: the president of the chamber asks deputies to rise from their seats to signal assent (bundestag.de, WD 1-013/24).\n- Unlike the constituent sitting of 21 March, the protocol of 17 May carries no attendance list; the Reichstag registry was later destroyed by wartime bombing, so no reliable record of who sat in the hall exists (bundestag.de, WD 1-013/24, citing Mergel).", "deep_lectura": "**Analyst read** *(inference — not stated verbatim in the record)*\n- A resolution with no enacting force is pure signal, and its value to the government lies abroad: parliament's blessing converts one cabinet's promise into 'Germany speaks with one voice'. The procedure — standing, visible, countable by eye — is built to manufacture exactly that picture.\n- For a deputy opposed to the regime the calculus of voting is inverted: a no cannot change the text or the government, but may change what happens tonight to colleagues in the cells. The resolution functions as a loyalty oath dressed as foreign policy, with hostages attached.\n- The declaration's very moderation is doing the work: by demanding only what every previous government demanded, it makes assent cheap and refusal look unpatriotic — and it buys the regime respectability abroad precisely while it consolidates at home.", "raw_outcome": "Entschließung des Reichstags zur Regierungserklärung („Friedensresolution“)", "source_url": "https://www.bundestag.de/resource/blob/1015024/WD-1-013-24-pdf.pdf", "outcome": "approved", "tally": {"for": 0, "against": 0, "abstain": 0}, "party_votes_canon": {"SPD": "for", "Z": "for", "BVP": "for", "DNVP": "for", "NSDAP": "for"}}]};
